---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { methodSpec, work, experiments } from '../data/experiments';
import SideDot from '../components/SideDot.astro';
import LockedRow from '../components/LockedRow.astro';
import spec from '../../method/method.md?raw';

// The thesis below is also the second sentence of method/method.md. Two copies of one
// claim drift, and README.md saying "these must match" is a comment, not a check —
// config that can drift from code will drift, so assert it. This fails the build at the
// only moment a human is looking, and it fails loudly rather than letting the site
// quietly stop quoting its own spec. Whitespace-collapsed because the markdown wraps
// the sentence across two lines.
const THESIS = 'Taste finds the simplest form; engineering proves the simplest solution.';
if (!spec.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').includes(THESIS)) {
  throw new Error(
    'method/method.md no longer contains the thesis sentence index.astro quotes:\n  ' + THESIS
  );
}

// `ENTRY_COUNT` was here, deriving the panel's Index row from the lists so a hand-written
// "8 case studies" couldn't go stale. The row is gone — it counted what was already on screen
// beside it — and the constant went with it rather than sitting unused as a live-looking
// reference to a row that no longer exists.
---

<Layout title="Andrew Shiau">
  <!-- THE PINNED PANEL + THE FEED (chooser: andrewshiau-redesign-directions round 2,
       concept 02; round 1 picks direction 12 + 10, hover-only motion, warm paper).
       The identity column stays put; the three ruled lists scroll past it.
       What this replaced: the statement ran full width at the top and the three lists ran
       under it, so the first screen was a sentence about me and the evidence started below
       the fold. Someone deciding whether to work with me was reading the claim before any
       of the work. Pinned, the claim is permanently on screen AND the artifacts start at
       the top of the reading column — the trade the old order couldn't make.
       One `.grid`, two children. Not two `.grid`s: `.grid + .grid` adds a --unit gap, and
       a sticky child positions against its own containing block, so the panel has to be a
       sibling of the feed or it has nothing to stick inside. -->
  <!-- `split` makes this one screen tall with the feed scrolling inside it, so the panel is
       pinned from the first pixel instead of drifting until sticky catches it. See THE BOX in
       global.css — including why the case-study panels deliberately stay sticky. -->
  <!-- The 1.5 units of air above this row used to be an inline margin here. It's a rule now —
       `main > .grid:first-child` in global.css — because the nine case studies never got the
       inline copy and opened flush against the top of the window. -->
  <div class="grid split">
    <div class="pin">
      <!-- NO MARK ABOVE THE NAME (chooser: andrewshiau-mark-options, Q3/05 — "as the row
           marker: it does a job"). A 44px figure sat here as the panel's masthead, and
           `Mark.astro` is deleted with it. The question the chooser asked was how little of the
           mark the page wants: a masthead asserts identity to a reader who already knows whose
           site they are on, while the same figure in the marker column tells them which half of
           the practice each row belongs to. So the mark moved to where it carries a fact — see
           SideDot.astro — and the panel opens on the sentence, which is the thing a hiring
           manager came for. Nothing replaced it: the panel's own hairline is the top edge. -->

      <!-- Same single-element statement as before, at the panel's measure: the name
           sentence with the two thesis clauses inline, each in its own hue. Still the only
           place colour enters body copy, because here the two halves of the practice ARE
           the subject. The nav wordmark already says the name, so this isn't a repeat. -->
      <h1 class="statement">Andrew Shiau is a frontend engineer who designs and builds,
        chasing simplicity. <span class="t-design">Taste finds the simplest form</span>,
        <span class="t-build">engineering proves the simplest solution</span>.</h1>

      <!-- The ledger — the "instrument" half of the pick. ONE row, and the one fact here that
           a reader can't get by looking at the page: what I am doing now. No invented metric,
           and no magnitude bars: a bar whose length encodes "8 case studies" against another
           encoding "124 recipes" compares nothing.
           `Currently`, not `Role`: the label was the category of the fact and the row read
           like a form field. What a reader deciding whether to work with me wants from this
           row is what I am doing NOW, and the label may as well say so.
           Two rows have come out of here. `Where` held only "At Amazon · 2023–present", which
           once the sentence names the employer is the same fact twice with a date attached.
           And `Index` counted the lists that are ON SCREEN to its right — it told a reader
           something they could see, in the panel that is permanently visible, at the cost of
           ~30px of the height THE BOX spends to stay pinned. Counting the page is what the
           page is for. -->
      <div class="pled">
        <div class="prow">
          <span class="pk">Currently</span>
          <span class="pv">Design systems engineer at Amazon, building a vibe design tool for
            the Amazon store.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WHERE TO REACH ME, at the foot of the column that never leaves the screen
           (chooser: andrewshiau-panel-shape, Q2/03 — "the colophon moves in", resolved as a
           split rather than a move; the reasoning is in the note above the closing band).
           These were previously in the colophon at the very bottom of the page, which meant
           the only way to reach me was to finish reading: the panel is permanently on screen,
           so this is the half of the old colophon that earns its place here.
           TWO labels, not one. All three links ran under `Elsewhere`, which made the address
           — the one thing here worth acting on — the first item in a list of places I post.
           `Contact` is a different ask from `Elsewhere`: one is how to reach me, the other is
           where else I am. `Contact` also matches the case-study panels, which have carried
           that exact label all along (see PanelHead.astro), so the index stops being the one
           page that names the same thing differently.
           No literal ↗ in the link text: `a[target]` already appends one in CSS, and writing
           it by hand rendered "github ↗ ↗". The colophon's own external link never had one,
           which is the pattern these should have followed. -->
      <div class="pfeet">
        <p class="pfoot">Contact
          <a href="mailto:hi@andrewshiau.com">hi@andrewshiau.com</a>
        </p>
        <p class="pfoot">Elsewhere
          <a href="https://github.com/halfcadence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github</a>
          <a href="https://www.instagram.com/half_cadence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">instagram</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The split's rule. An empty element because it belongs to neither side; see
         `.splitrule` in global.css for why it isn't a border on the panel. -->
    <div class="splitrule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- Method FIRST, above the evidence (placement pick 04). The statement in the panel
           IS this spec's opening claim, so the thing that states it sits beside it; a
           reader who wants the artifacts scrolls one row. One row, not a section of prose —
           the cost of putting a claim before the evidence is a manifesto, and one list row
           can't be one. Label only, no dek: Work and Experiments carry none either.
           IT IS NOW THE SAME ROW AS EVERY OTHER — title, leader, marker, and nothing else.
           Annotation: "i think we should cut the design / build sections here and just put
           title." The row used to carry the two procedures as nested sub-rows (a kicker, a
           title and a blurb each), which made the Method row four times the height of a Work
           row and put the most prose on the page in the one place a reader hasn't decided to
           read yet. It was also the last thing on the index that still had a blurb, after the
           mono pass took blurbs off the eight rows below it — so the index had one row shaped
           differently from the other nine for no reason a reader could see.
           The procedures aren't lost; they are what `/method/` is, one click away, where the
           `.duo` states them side by side at full size. An index says what's there. -->
      <h2>Method</h2>
      <div class="matrix">
        <a class="entry" href={methodSpec.href}>
          {/* No numeral: there is one of it, and a set "01" promises an "02" that never comes.
              The row still opens with the same 3ch offset as its neighbours so the three lists
              share one title axis — see `.entry.entry-solo` in global.css. */}
          <span class="eh">{methodSpec.title}</span>
          <span class="ends"></span>
          <span class="em">
            <SideDot side={methodSpec.side} />
            <span class="smeta">{methodSpec.meta}</span>
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Work + Experiments — ONE reference list, the same ruled table twice: number ·
           title · blurb · marker. This replaced a calendar scatter in both sections. The
           scatter composed better in isolation and indexed worse: both sections used it, so
           only the heading distinguished them, and the page ran ~2.5 screens with neither
           list ever whole in view. An index's job is to show what's there.
           In the feed column the row runs on three tracks instead of twelve — see
           `.feed .entry` in global.css; same device, narrower measure. -->
      <h2>Work</h2>
      <div class="matrix">
        {work.map((x) => (
          x.locked ? (
            /* THE LOCKED ROW opens in place: the gate is this row, expanded under itself, so
               the click and the ask happen in the same object and the reader never leaves the
               index (chooser: andrewshiau-gate-options, option 05). It renders two siblings —
               the row and its gate cell — which is why it is a component rather than a branch
               inside the markup here: a `<form>` cannot live inside the `<a>`, so the pair has
               to be emitted together, and `/gate/` renders the same pair already open.
               It looks like every other row — same numeral, same title, same arrow. The lock
               glyph and the `Password` marker it used to carry are gone; the reasoning is in
               the component and beside `.entry .arrowc` in global.css. */
            <LockedRow x={x} />
          ) : (
            <a class:list={['entry', { accent: x.accent }]} href={x.href}
               target={x.external ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={x.external ? 'noopener' : undefined}>
              <span class="en">{x.n}</span>
              <span class="eh">{x.title}</span>
              {/* The leader. An empty span because the space between the title and the meta
                  IS the device — see `.entry .ends` in global.css. */}
              <span class="ends"></span>
              <span class="em">
                <SideDot side={x.side} />
                <span class="smeta">{x.meta}</span>
              </span>
            </a>
          )
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Experiments</h2>
      <div class="matrix">
        {experiments.map((x) => (
          <a class:list={['entry', { accent: x.accent }]} href={x.href}
             target={x.external ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={x.external ? 'noopener' : undefined}>
            <span class="en">{x.n}</span>
            <span class="eh">{x.title}</span>
            <span class="ends"></span>
            <span class="em">
              <SideDot side={x.side} />
              <span class="smeta">{x.meta}</span>
            </span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- THE CLOSING BAND — placed on the grid, not stacked full-width. Label column at the
       left, facts as aligned satellites; the right columns stay empty. `.colo-rule`'s 2px
       line is what closes the page. It is also what THE BOX brings up: chooser Q3/04 asked for
       the closing sequence to arrive as one composed screen, and the box does that by geometry
       rather than by a snap — collapsing the split to one screen leaves the page exactly one
       scroll past the feed, so the only place it can go is the end. Measured at seven
       viewports: colophon and copyright always land together. See THE CLOSING BAND in
       global.css for the snap that was tried here first and what measuring it removed.
       It is SPLIT with the panel rather than moved into it (chooser Q2/03, "the colophon
       moves in", plus the ask to promote the contact links). Both picks were right about a
       different half, and moving all of it either way lost something:
       - Reaching me shouldn't require finishing the page, so github / instagram / the
         address went UP into the panel, where they're permanently on screen.
       - The page still has to end. Take the whole colophon into the panel and the feed just
         stops: no 2px rule, nothing for the handoff to land on, and the last row of
         Experiments runs into the footer.
       What's left here is what a colophon is actually for — facts about the page and its
       lineage, read after the work, not links you need while deciding. Which also fixes a
       mislabel: this block called itself "Colophon" while its first column was contact
       details, and now the label is true.
       Nothing is printed twice. github and instagram are in the panel and only there. -->
  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*3)">
    <div class="colo-rule"></div>
    <div class="colo-lab">Colophon</div>
    <div class="colo c1">
      <span class="ck">Before AI</span>
      <a href="https://2018.andrewshiau.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">portfolio2018</a>
    </div>
    <!-- A colophon states what the page is set in, so it has to be true. It said "Graphik,
         self-hosted" for one deploy after the mono pass shipped, which made the one fact a reader
         could check against the page itself the one fact that was wrong.
         "self-hosted" is OUT. Annotation: "is 'self-hosted' relevant here? if its design details
         should it be all design details" — and self-hosting is a delivery decision, not a design
         one. It sat in a list of design facts as the only piece of infrastructure trivia, which is
         the inconsistency the annotation names. The rest of the line is now all one kind of fact:
         the typeface, the size, the measure — what a reader can see and check.
         Kept short deliberately. A colophon that lists everything is a spec sheet; this is the
         three decisions the page is actually made of. -->
    <div class="colo c2">
      <span class="ck">Set in</span>
      IBM Plex Mono at 15/28, one size, 62 characters.
    </div>
    <!-- THE GRID FACT, moved here out of the footer (annotation: "maybe this can be in colophon
         and be like 'grid // 12 columns. press cmd+g'"). It belongs with the other two: a
         colophon states what the page is made of, and "12 columns" is exactly that — where it
         was, it sat alone in a footer bar whose only other content had already been deleted.
         The keypress is stated only where a keyboard exists. On a touch device `.kb` is hidden
         and `.tou` shows the fact without the instruction, which is the rule the footer already
         had — an affordance the device can't reach shouldn't be told to the reader as a fact. -->
    <div class="colo c3">
      <span class="ck">Grid</span>
      <span class="kb">12 columns. Press <code>⌥G</code> to see it.</span>
      <span class="tou">12 columns.</span>
    </div>
    <!-- THE STEERING FILE, linked (annotation: "we should encode this in site steering, and maybe
         promote site steering to the colophon — i think the working in public thing is rly cool").
         STYLE.md is the file that decides everything the other three colophon facts state: it is
         where the typeface, the 12 columns and the one-mechanism-per-job rule are written down,
         and it is what an agent reads before touching this site. Linking it is the same move
         /method/ already makes with its own three markdown files — the page shows its source
         rather than describing it.
         It is a `blob` link to the repo, not a rendered page: the file is 800 lines of RFC-2119
         steering with contrast measurements in it, and rendering that as a site page would make
         it look like content. On GitHub it looks like what it is, which is the honest version of
         working in public. -->
    <div class="colo c4">
      <span class="ck">Steered by</span>
      <a href="https://github.com/halfcadence/andrewshiau.com/blob/main/STYLE.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">STYLE.md</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
